{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "276",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 30s with a history of right breast cancer, stage IIIa (cT2N2M0). She underwent neoadjuvant chemotherapy followed by right modified radical mastectomy and postoperative radiotherapy. After radiotherapy, she noticed a pruritic erythema lesion with multiple nodules over the operation site of the right breast. The lesion did not respond to initial topical treatments. Three months later (1 year after initial cancer diagnosis), she presented to dermatology for evaluation of skin erythema over her right breast.\n\nA well-demarcated, warm, erythematous plaque, 15 × 12 cm, was seen over the right side of the anterior chest wall around her operation scar. Scattered within the erythema were multiple palpable small nodules.\n\n- Immunohistochemistry:   Negative for estrogen receptor (ER), progesterone receptor (PR), and human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 (HER2)- Skin Biopsy:  Multiple emboli of carcinoma cells in dermal and subcutaneous lymphatic vessels- Images:  Image Title: Postmastectomy Clinical Images Before and After Treatment  Image Description A: Right mastectomy scar with a large well-defined area of erythema and multiple, scattered small nodules  Image Description B: Nearly complete remission of skin lesions after treatment",
